<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Bronze Age Collapse</h1><p class="path-tagline">How a connected world of great kings fell apart around 1200 BCE, and what grew in its place.</p><p class="path-intro">In the fourteenth and thirteenth centuries BCE the eastern Mediterranean and the Near East formed something like an international system: great kings in Egypt, Hatti, Mitanni, Babylon and Assyria wrote to one another as brothers, exchanged gifts and princesses, and fought over Syria. Within a few decades around 1200 BCE much of it was gone. Palaces burned, the Hittite Empire disappeared, cities such as Ugarit were never rebuilt, and Egypt survived only in weakened form. This path follows the great powers, the crisis and the new world of iron and smaller kingdoms that came after.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Late Bronze Age</span><span class="path-step-why">An age of great territorial powers, Egypt, the Hittites, Mitanni, Babylon and Assyria, linked by diplomacy, trade and chariot warfare.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amarna-letters.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Work · 14th century BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Amarna letters</span><span class="path-step-why">Clay tablets of the 14th century BCE in which the pharaohs and the kings of Babylon, Mitanni and the Hittites wrote to one another in Akkadian.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1274 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Battle of Kadesh</span><span class="path-step-why">Ramesses II and Muwatalli II fought one of antiquity's best-documented chariot battles in 1274 BCE, and their kingdoms later made peace.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/hittite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 1650 BCE – 1178 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Hittite Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Anatolia's great power rivalled Egypt for Syria and left the earliest peace treaty surviving in both sides' versions, until Hattusa burned around 1180 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1800 BCE – 1185 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Ugarit</span><span class="path-step-why">A rich port whose last letters report enemy ships off the coast; it was sacked and burned around 1185 BCE and never rebuilt.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sea-peoples.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · People · c. 1177 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Sea Peoples</span><span class="path-step-why">Seafaring raiders of the end of the Late Bronze Age; Egyptian records tell of Ramesses III defeating them on land and sea around 1177 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event · c. 1200 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Late Bronze Age collapse</span><span class="path-step-why">Drought, famine, migrations, unrest and broken trade: the debated causes of a collapse that ended the Hittite state and weakened Egypt.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/philistines"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philistines.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · People · 12th–7th centuries BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Philistines</span><span class="path-step-why">Probably among the Sea Peoples, they settled the coast of Canaan in the 12th century BCE and formed a league of five cities.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iron-age.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Idea · from c. 1200 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Iron Age</span><span class="path-step-why">Iron replaced bronze for tools and weapons from around 1200 BCE, in an age of new kingdoms, hillforts and first written records.</span></span></a></li></ol>
